// Translate in place: replace the //RS lines below with the 1:1 Jet port (keep `// Rust: <file>:<line>` anchors).
//RS /// The one signal-handler mechanism (#2027, I8 + I9).
//RS ///
//RS /// This file is the single home for every signal fact: the pending interrupt
//RS /// count, typed process-signal bits, the platform handler, the arm path, and
//RS /// the consumption rules. Nothing outside this file may declare a second
//RS /// signal mark function or a second install path.
//RS ///
//RS /// Three tiers reach it and none of them restates it:
//RS ///   * AOT — `Prelude/CoreLib/Top/FSIoEnvOsTesting.rs`'s `jet_os_interrupt`,
//RS ///     which the generated program embeds with the process/filesystem adapter
//RS ///     closure (`needs_process || needs_fs_runtime`);
//RS ///     the full dispatcher remains the one shared implementation.
//RS ///   * the resident Cranelift host — `jet-jit/src/CoreHost.rs`, via
//RS ///     `jet_codegen::interrupt_runtime`;
//RS ///   * the TIR evaluator ambient — `Codegen/TIR/eval/mod.rs`, via
//RS ///     `crate::interrupt_runtime`.
//RS ///
//RS /// The last two share ONE compiled instance (`jet-codegen/src/lib.rs`,
//RS /// `pub mod interrupt_runtime`), so the `jet` binary has one pending state,
//RS /// one signal disposition, and one arm result no matter which tier is running.
//RS /// `signal(SIGINT, …)` replaces the process handler, so a second instance
//RS /// would silently disarm the first tier's queue.
//RS ///
//RS /// A tier supplies only its Ctrl-C handler storage and invocation adapter.
//RS /// Typed process signals use a shared cancellation callback instead: it looks
//RS /// up the current root task control when delivered, so a resident run cannot
//RS /// keep a prior run alive.
//RS ///
//RS /// `#Shield` never enters this file, by construction. A shield defers a
//RS /// *cooperative* interrupt — a cancel or a blown deadline — at the wait points
//RS /// of the shielded task (`Prelude/Scheduler.rs::jet_scheduler_shielded`). A
//RS /// process signal marks cancellation through the same task-control carrier;
//RS /// the cancellation is delivered by the scheduler at its next unshielded wait
//RS /// point.
//RS pub fn jet_interrupt_poll_interval() -> std::time::Duration {
//RS     std::time::Duration::from_millis(10)
//RS }
